WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.270 --> 00:00:02.670
- [Narrator] Chinese
firms fully or partly own

00:00:02.670 --> 00:00:05.940
and operate all these ports
outside China's borders.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:08.520
There's 95 of them on this map.

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:11.010
Meanwhile, US companies don't operate

00:00:11.010 --> 00:00:12.870
any major ports abroad.

00:00:12.870 --> 00:00:14.850
For China, these ports have been part

00:00:14.850 --> 00:00:17.850
of a long-term effort to
build global infrastructure.

00:00:17.850 --> 00:00:19.500
Chinese firms have spent years

00:00:19.500 --> 00:00:21.840
and billions of dollars to build, operate,

00:00:21.840 --> 00:00:24.030
and develop ports around the world.

00:00:24.030 --> 00:00:26.910
American officials view
China's expanding network

00:00:26.910 --> 00:00:28.920
as an increasing security threat,

00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.400
posing significant economic
and even military risks.

00:00:32.400 --> 00:00:34.800
So what has the US done to counter this?

00:00:34.800 --> 00:00:37.820
Block China from making
more deals to keep ports

00:00:37.820 --> 00:00:39.930
in so-called friendly hands,

00:00:39.930 --> 00:00:42.693
but the US has had mixed success.

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:45.780
In the past decade,

00:00:45.780 --> 00:00:47.790
China has spent a trillion dollars

00:00:47.790 --> 00:00:49.560
on its Belt And Road Initiative,

00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.690
a global infrastructure project designed

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:53.220
to boost China's economy

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:55.590
and political clout through
government-backed investments

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.090
in dozens of countries.

00:00:57.090 --> 00:01:00.000
- The scale of China's trading enterprise

00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.500
is really staggering.

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:03.150
- [Narrator] Isaac
Kardon is a senior fellow

00:01:03.150 --> 00:01:06.300
at the Carnegie Endowment
studying China's maritime affairs.

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:08.910
He and a colleague spent
years building a data set

00:01:08.910 --> 00:01:12.240
of all Chinese owned and
operated ports around the world.

00:01:12.240 --> 00:01:16.110
It currently includes 95 ports
across dozens of countries.

00:01:16.110 --> 00:01:18.150
- The strategic motivations for China,

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:21.120
I think, are fundamentally defensive.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.270
They feel vulnerable to the United States

00:01:24.270 --> 00:01:26.640
and its alliance network
that they could be cut

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:31.290
out of the global economic
system in some fundamental way.

00:01:31.290 --> 00:01:34.320
And so I think the drive to
make themselves indispensable

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.080
to it and central to it is in part a way

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:40.140
of insulating themselves
against that vulnerability.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:41.220
- [Narrator] Ports are considered

00:01:41.220 --> 00:01:42.720
national security infrastructure

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:44.670
by the US government
because they're central

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:47.520
to the flow of essential
goods like steel and food.

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:49.860
But commercial ports are dual purpose.

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:51.720
They're useful both economically,

00:01:51.720 --> 00:01:53.880
China can give its
vessels priority handling,

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:56.580
and lower docking fees, and militarily,

00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.490
docks are used by navies
to refuel, restock,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:00.930
and re-arm ships.

00:02:00.930 --> 00:02:03.510
In recent years, the US
has worked to keep China

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:06.603
from expanding this network
in a few strategic regions.

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:10.320
China has three main shipping
and logistics companies

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:11.700
that own most of its stakes

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:14.730
in overseas ports, Cosco Shipping Ports,

00:02:14.730 --> 00:02:17.550
China Merchants Port and Hutchison Ports.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:19.620
Cosco and China Merchants are both owned

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:21.210
by China's central government,

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:23.400
while Hutchinson is privately owned.

00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.120
Cosco is an investor in several
key US ports, Los Angeles,

00:02:27.120 --> 00:02:29.220
Long Beach, and Seattle.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:32.430
But in 2017, when Cosco
almost took over operation

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.140
of a Long Beach container terminal,

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:36.390
US regulators stepped in.

00:02:36.390 --> 00:02:38.070
The Trump administration blocked them

00:02:38.070 --> 00:02:41.310
from getting control out of
national security concerns.

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.920
Ultimately, Cosco sold the terminal,

00:02:43.920 --> 00:02:47.250
but Chinese firms are still
a major presence at US ports.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:50.160
Cosco still has a major stake
in another container terminal

00:02:50.160 --> 00:02:52.710
at Long Beach from a preexisting deal,

00:02:52.710 --> 00:02:54.360
and lots of port infrastructure

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:55.800
like the cranes that move cargo

00:02:55.800 --> 00:02:58.200
from ship to shore is made in China.

00:02:58.200 --> 00:03:00.510
Lately, some US officials have argued

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.490
that's a security concern too.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:03.930
- There's growing suspicion

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:08.100
that anything which is
controlled by China is used

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:09.813
to spy on the United States.

00:03:10.989 --> 00:03:13.500
There's been procedures at
the US government to see

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:17.280
whether the cranes at
American ports, which are made

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:22.280
in China, are used to spy on
the operations of the port.

00:03:22.350 --> 00:03:25.170
- In Europe, the US has also
been trying to block China

00:03:25.170 --> 00:03:26.880
from expanding its stake in ports

00:03:26.880 --> 00:03:29.220
and controlling the flow
of goods to the continent.

00:03:29.220 --> 00:03:32.460
But US officials have had mixed
success convincing countries

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:34.770
that security concerns
outweigh the benefits

00:03:34.770 --> 00:03:36.600
of Chinese state investment.

00:03:36.600 --> 00:03:38.790
In Hamburg, Germany's largest port,

00:03:38.790 --> 00:03:41.490
Cosco planned to buy a 35% stake

00:03:41.490 --> 00:03:43.560
in one of the port's container terminals.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:47.430
Government officials reduced
the potential stake to 24.9%

00:03:47.430 --> 00:03:49.590
but still faced criticism from the US

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:51.390
and many in the German government.

00:03:51.390 --> 00:03:53.730
The country's chancellor
ultimately permitted the deal

00:03:53.730 --> 00:03:56.910
to move forward, but the
offer is still pending review.

00:03:56.910 --> 00:04:00.334
- The United States would
advise and press any country

00:04:00.334 --> 00:04:05.334
in the world not to accept
Chinese investments.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:07.200
It's a big dilemma for countries

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.230
because the Chinese
have the cash to invest,

00:04:10.230 --> 00:04:12.870
which means this creates a lot of jobs,

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:16.830
and it also improves the
outputs of those countries.

00:04:16.830 --> 00:04:18.510
- [Narrator] The Hamburg
deal though continues

00:04:18.510 --> 00:04:20.820
to face opposition which
will make it difficult

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:21.840
to move forward.

00:04:21.840 --> 00:04:24.930
- In the past, such small investment

00:04:24.930 --> 00:04:26.280
wouldn't even make news.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:29.400
It's just indicative of
how tense the relations

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.710
of the Western world are with Beijing

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.960
- [Narrator] A similar
scene played out in Croatia,

00:04:33.960 --> 00:04:36.780
but in that case, the
US was more successful.

00:04:36.780 --> 00:04:38.580
It used behind the scenes diplomacy

00:04:38.580 --> 00:04:40.650
to have Croatia cancel a deal it had made

00:04:40.650 --> 00:04:43.200
with Chinese firms looking to
operate a container terminal

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:45.060
in Rijeka and give the contract

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:47.790
to a Danish company, Maersk, instead.

00:04:47.790 --> 00:04:49.530
Rijeka is strategically valuable

00:04:49.530 --> 00:04:51.510
because it's close to the Suez Canal

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:54.030
and has access to
central European markets.

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:56.850
The US government was particularly
concerned about the deal

00:04:56.850 --> 00:04:59.700
because the US Navy and
NATO sometimes use the port

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.790
to move military equipment
in and out of Europe.

00:05:02.790 --> 00:05:04.950
China could have made that more difficult.

00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:09.630
- What Western intelligence
officials are saying is

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:13.770
that by controlling this
terminals, they could eventually

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:17.820
have a navy installation
there, in the future.

00:05:17.820 --> 00:05:20.130
I don't think this will be the case,

00:05:20.130 --> 00:05:22.920
especially in any European
point in time soon,

00:05:22.920 --> 00:05:25.140
but it could happen in Africa.

00:05:25.140 --> 00:05:26.970
- [Narrator] The US has
arrangements with dozens

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:29.550
of military bases in
Africa, including several

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.760
on the continent's west,
north, and east coasts.

00:05:32.760 --> 00:05:35.700
China has just one base in Djibouti,

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.790
but while US firms don't own
commercial ports in Africa,

00:05:38.790 --> 00:05:40.740
Chinese firms own many.

00:05:40.740 --> 00:05:43.500
US officials have been
concerned about China building

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:46.020
on the relationships it's
formed through commercial ports

00:05:46.020 --> 00:05:49.260
to establish additional permanent
military bases in Africa,

00:05:49.260 --> 00:05:51.240
especially on the Atlantic ocean,

00:05:51.240 --> 00:05:53.610
waters the US considers home turf.

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.070
- The thing I think I'm most worried about

00:05:56.070 --> 00:06:00.600
is this military base
on the Atlantic coast,

00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:02.010
and where they have the most traction

00:06:02.010 --> 00:06:04.860
for that today is in Equatorial Guinea.

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:06.180
- [Narrator] In Equatorial Guinea,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:08.580
the US has been trying to
convince country officials

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:11.130
to reject China's bid
to build a military base

00:06:11.130 --> 00:06:12.300
in the city of Bata,

00:06:12.300 --> 00:06:14.820
where China already has a
commercial port deep enough

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:16.320
for naval vessels to dock.

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:19.890
- It's easy to put, install naval facility

00:06:19.890 --> 00:06:21.705
at a commercial port.

00:06:21.705 --> 00:06:22.950
If the port infrastructure is there,

00:06:22.950 --> 00:06:25.230
it can be used by a navy ship.

00:06:25.230 --> 00:06:29.400
There are, I think, real concerns

00:06:29.400 --> 00:06:33.022
that eventually you will
see Chinese warships docking

00:06:33.022 --> 00:06:35.160
at that port.

00:06:35.160 --> 00:06:37.440
- [Narrator] If it were to
establish a military base there,

00:06:37.440 --> 00:06:39.840
Equatorial Guinea would
be China's first base

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:40.830
on the Atlantic,

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:43.170
which would enable
Chinese warships to re-arm

00:06:43.170 --> 00:06:45.690
opposite the East Coast of the US.

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:47.940
US officials have gone
to Equatorial Guinea

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:51.030
to persuade leaders there
to reject China's overtures

00:06:51.030 --> 00:06:53.310
but it's unclear whether
the diplomatic outreach

00:06:53.310 --> 00:06:55.800
will fend off a Chinese
naval presence there.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:57.900
- This will be something

00:06:57.900 --> 00:07:00.930
that US officials are watching closely.

00:07:00.930 --> 00:07:04.050
Beijing hopes that it could

00:07:04.050 --> 00:07:08.370
in the future have a military presence

00:07:08.370 --> 00:07:11.520
in terminals and ports that it controls.

00:07:11.520 --> 00:07:14.850
- Whether it is or isn't
defensive is almost irrelevant.

00:07:14.850 --> 00:07:16.290
It has security consequences.

00:07:16.290 --> 00:07:18.180
We need to be attuned to those.

00:07:18.180 --> 00:07:20.610
We need to focus on what
we're trying to accomplish

00:07:20.610 --> 00:07:24.450
and maybe that means we
do need to counter China

00:07:24.450 --> 00:07:26.370
at various turns

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:29.580
but that competition is
not gonna be one for one,

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:30.960
you get a port, we get a port.

00:07:30.960 --> 00:07:32.520
It's more like what can we do

00:07:32.520 --> 00:07:35.070
to secure our own maritime
trade and transport,

00:07:35.070 --> 00:07:36.720
because ultimately that's
what they're doing.

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:39.780
It just has negative externalities

00:07:39.780 --> 00:07:44.253
for our own ability to feel
secure in maritime trade.

